1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,801
On the ninth of March, 1974, a
sixty-one-year-old bookseller flew from

2
00:00:04,801 --> 00:00:09,373
Japan to a small island in the
Philippines, walked into the jungle, and

3
00:00:09,373 --> 00:00:13,545
read a short statement aloud to a man
standing at attention in a patched

4
00:00:13,545 --> 00:00:14,924
uniform.

5
00:00:14,974 --> 00:00:19,379
The statement said that the Japanese
Fourteenth Area Army had been dissolved.

6
00:00:19,379 --> 00:00:23,937
That all combat activity was to cease
immediately. That the man was relieved of

7
00:00:23,937 --> 00:00:25,363
his duties.

8
00:00:25,413 --> 00:00:30,748
The man listening was Second Lieutenant
Hiroo Onoda. He was fifty-one years old.

9
00:00:30,748 --> 00:00:35,870
He had been fighting the Second World War,
alone, for the last eighteen months, and

10
00:00:35,870 --> 00:00:39,581
with others for the twenty-seven years
before that.

11
00:00:40,131 --> 00:00:45,911
He had heard, many times, that the war was
over. He had read leaflets saying so. He

12
00:00:45,911 --> 00:00:50,312
had heard it from loudspeakers, from
search parties, from his own family's

13
00:00:50,312 --> 00:00:55,541
voices played over the jungle on a
recording. He had dismissed all of it.

14
00:00:55,591 --> 00:01:00,754
What he had been waiting for was this. An
order, from the officer who gave him the

15
00:01:00,754 --> 00:01:03,712
original one, delivered in person.

16
00:01:04,262 --> 00:01:09,556
That officer was a bookseller now. He had
been found in a shop in Japan and asked to

17
00:01:09,556 --> 00:01:11,842
put the uniform back on.

18
00:01:12,692 --> 00:01:16,412
When he finished reading, Onoda took the
bolt out of his rifle.

19
00:01:18,912 --> 00:01:24,080
Hiroo Onoda was born in March 1922, in a
village in Wakayama Prefecture. Before the

20
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:29,195
war he worked for a trading company in
China, selling lacquerware. He was good at

21
00:01:29,195 --> 00:01:34,309
it. He had a gift for languages and an
ordinary young man's life, and when he was

22
00:01:34,309 --> 00:01:38,697
drafted at twenty he was, by every
measure, unremarkable.

23
00:01:38,747 --> 00:01:41,953
What made him different was where he was
sent to train.

24
00:01:42,503 --> 00:01:46,864
The Nakano School, in Tokyo, trained men
for intelligence and unconventional

25
00:01:46,864 --> 00:01:51,745
warfare. Not infantry. Guerrilla
operations, sabotage, intelligence

26
00:01:51,745 --> 00:01:56,314
gathering, and above all the doctrine of
holding out behind enemy lines for as long

27
00:01:56,314 --> 00:02:01,194
as it took. The school taught its men that
there were no circumstances in which they

28
00:02:01,194 --> 00:02:06,075
were to be captured, and no circumstances
in which they were to kill themselves.

29
00:02:06,075 --> 00:02:11,305
Both were failures. Survival and continued
resistance was the entire point.

30
00:02:11,355 --> 00:02:16,068
That training matters more than anything
else in this story. Onoda was not a

31
00:02:16,068 --> 00:02:21,168
frightened conscript who got lost. He was
a man specifically trained to do exactly

32
00:02:21,168 --> 00:02:25,992
what he then did, by an institution that
considered doing it for decades a success

33
00:02:25,992 --> 00:02:28,116
rather than a tragedy.

34
00:02:28,766 --> 00:02:34,064
In December 1944 he was sent to Lubang
Island, in the Philippines. The island is

35
00:02:34,064 --> 00:02:39,532
small, about thirty kilometres long. The
Allied advance was already closing on the

36
00:02:39,532 --> 00:02:44,716
Philippines, and Onoda's orders were to
destroy the island's airstrip and pier, to

37
00:02:44,716 --> 00:02:47,686
harass any landing, and to hold.

38
00:02:47,736 --> 00:02:53,130
His commanding officer was Major Yoshimi
Taniguchi. The order Taniguchi gave him,

39
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:58,984
before he left, was specific. Under no
circumstances was he to surrender. Under

40
00:02:58,984 --> 00:03:04,436
no circumstances was he to take his own
life. He was to hold out, however long it

41
00:03:04,436 --> 00:03:08,114
took, and Taniguchi would come back for
him.

42
00:03:08,764 --> 00:03:13,257
Three months later the Americans landed on
Lubang. The Japanese garrison was

43
00:03:13,257 --> 00:03:18,701
destroyed in days. Onoda took to the hills
with three other men. Private First Class

44
00:03:18,701 --> 00:03:24,337
Yuichi Akatsu, Corporal Shoichi Shimada,
and Private First Class Kinshichi Kozuka.

45
00:03:24,387 --> 00:03:29,537
Four men, in a jungle, on a small island,
with an order to hold.

46
00:03:30,787 --> 00:03:36,369
The war ended five months later. Nobody on
Lubang's hills believed it.

47
00:03:37,219 --> 00:03:42,546
Understanding why requires putting aside
the obvious explanation. Onoda was not

48
00:03:42,546 --> 00:03:47,994
insane and he was not stupid. He was
applying a piece of reasoning that was,

49
00:03:47,994 --> 00:03:52,036
given what he had been taught, almost
airtight.

50
00:03:52,086 --> 00:03:56,583
He had been trained in deception
operations. He knew, professionally, that

51
00:03:56,583 --> 00:04:01,080
an enemy will tell a holdout force that
the war is over in order to flush it out.

52
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:05,379
So every announcement that the war was
over was, to him, evidence that someone

53
00:04:05,379 --> 00:04:10,024
wanted him out of the hills. The louder
the announcement, the more effort behind

54
00:04:10,024 --> 00:04:14,624
it, the more it confirmed that he mattered
enough to be worth deceiving.

55
00:04:14,674 --> 00:04:19,319
In late 1945, leaflets were dropped on the
island carrying a surrender order from

56
00:04:19,319 --> 00:04:24,237
General Tomoyuki Yamashita. Onoda and the
others examined them closely and found

57
00:04:24,237 --> 00:04:28,718
what they considered errors, phrasing a
Japanese staff officer would not have

58
00:04:28,718 --> 00:04:32,784
used. They concluded the leaflets were
forgeries.

59
00:04:32,834 --> 00:04:37,296
By their own logic, they were right to be
suspicious. By any other measure, they had

60
00:04:37,296 --> 00:04:40,253
just talked themselves out of the end of
the war.

61
00:04:41,103 --> 00:04:46,960
More came. Newspapers left in the jungle.
Photographs. Letters from families.

62
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:51,728
Loudspeakers. In one attempt, recordings
of relatives' voices were played from

63
00:04:51,728 --> 00:04:56,237
aircraft. Onoda listened to his own
brother's voice calling him and concluded

64
00:04:56,237 --> 00:05:00,941
the recording had been made under duress,
or spliced, or imitated.

65
00:05:00,991 --> 00:05:06,211
There is no argument you can make to a man
who has decided that every argument is

66
00:05:06,211 --> 00:05:08,301
part of the trap.

67
00:05:09,551 --> 00:05:14,228
So they stayed. They lived on bananas,
coconuts, and rice they took from the

68
00:05:14,228 --> 00:05:19,056
island's farms. They killed cattle for
meat. They built shelters in the rainy

69
00:05:19,056 --> 00:05:23,683
season and moved constantly in the dry.
They maintained their rifles with palm oil

70
00:05:23,683 --> 00:05:29,012
and animal fat for twenty-nine years, and
the rifles still worked at the end of it.

71
00:05:29,062 --> 00:05:33,998
And they fought. This is the part most
retellings leave out, and it should not be

72
00:05:33,998 --> 00:05:35,346
left out.

73
00:05:36,396 --> 00:05:41,434
To Onoda, Lubang was an active theatre of
war and its inhabitants were an enemy

74
00:05:41,434 --> 00:05:47,200
population. The farmers whose cattle he
took were enemy. The police who came

75
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:52,784
looking for him were enemy. Over the
course of nearly three decades, his group

76
00:05:52,784 --> 00:05:58,232
is estimated to have killed around thirty
Filipinos and wounded many more.

77
00:05:58,282 --> 00:06:02,307
They were not soldiers. They were
islanders, farming an island they had

78
00:06:02,307 --> 00:06:06,092
always farmed, being shot at by men
conducting a war that had ended before

79
00:06:06,092 --> 00:06:08,023
some of them were born.

80
00:06:08,873 --> 00:06:13,524
Any honest version of this story has to
carry that. The loyalty people admire in

81
00:06:13,524 --> 00:06:18,803
Onoda and the deaths on Lubang are not two
separate facts. They are the same fact.

82
00:06:20,803 --> 00:06:26,257
The group shrank. Akatsu, the youngest,
walked away from the others in 1949 and

83
00:06:26,257 --> 00:06:31,371
gave himself up in 1950. The remaining
three treated his defection as proof that

84
00:06:31,371 --> 00:06:34,107
he had been captured and turned.

85
00:06:34,157 --> 00:06:39,857
In May 1954, Shimada was shot dead in a
clash with a Filipino search party on a

86
00:06:39,857 --> 00:06:41,306
beach.

87
00:06:42,156 --> 00:06:47,729
In October 1972, twenty-seven years after
the war ended, Kinshichi Kozuka was killed

88
00:06:47,729 --> 00:06:52,472
in a firefight with Philippine police
while the two men were burning a farmer's

89
00:06:52,472 --> 00:06:56,972
rice stores. Kozuka had been with Onoda
since 1944.

90
00:06:58,222 --> 00:07:02,954
After that, Onoda was alone. He was fifty
years old and he had been in the jungle

91
00:07:02,954 --> 00:07:08,844
for most of his adult life. He kept up the
patrols. He kept the rifle clean. He kept,

92
00:07:08,844 --> 00:07:13,425
as far as anyone can tell, to the schedule
of a soldier on active operations, with

93
00:07:13,425 --> 00:07:15,388
nobody left to see him do it.

94
00:07:17,588 --> 00:07:22,378
Kozuka's death put Onoda back in the
Japanese newspapers. Search parties went

95
00:07:22,378 --> 00:07:24,845
out again and found nothing.

96
00:07:24,895 --> 00:07:30,056
And then, in early 1974, a
twenty-four-year-old university dropout

97
00:07:30,056 --> 00:07:33,825
named Norio Suzuki flew to the
Philippines.

98
00:07:34,375 --> 00:07:39,841
Suzuki was not a soldier, a journalist, or
a government official. He was a traveller,

99
00:07:39,841 --> 00:07:44,740
and he had set himself a list of three
things to find. In his own description, he

100
00:07:44,740 --> 00:07:50,865
was looking for Lieutenant Onoda, a panda,
and the Abominable Snowman. In that order.

101
00:07:51,715 --> 00:07:56,899
He went to Lubang alone, with camping
gear. He did not bring a search party, a

102
00:07:56,899 --> 00:08:01,942
loudspeaker, or a weapon. He walked into
the jungle and waited.

103
00:08:01,992 --> 00:08:08,276
On the twentieth of February 1974, after
four days, Onoda found him.

104
00:08:09,526 --> 00:08:14,637
Consider that from Onoda's position.
Twenty-nine years of search parties, armed

105
00:08:14,637 --> 00:08:19,858
patrols, aircraft, and broadcasts. And
then one young man sitting alone in the

106
00:08:19,858 --> 00:08:24,612
jungle with no equipment and no backup,
apparently wanting to talk.

107
00:08:25,262 --> 00:08:29,851
Onoda said later that Suzuki had come to
the island to listen to the feelings of a

108
00:08:29,851 --> 00:08:34,679
Japanese soldier. Suzuki asked him why he
would not come out.

109
00:08:34,729 --> 00:08:39,963
They spent days together. They became
friends, which is a strange and very human

110
00:08:39,963 --> 00:08:45,703
detail. Onoda explained his position. He
would not come out, because he had not

111
00:08:45,703 --> 00:08:51,049
been ordered out. He had received an order
in 1944 from Major Taniguchi, and only

112
00:08:51,049 --> 00:08:56,295
Major Taniguchi could rescind it. Anything
else was enemy deception.

113
00:08:56,345 --> 00:09:00,469
Suzuki took photographs as proof and flew
home.

114
00:09:01,019 --> 00:09:05,505
The Japanese government went looking for
Taniguchi. They found him working as a

115
00:09:05,505 --> 00:09:06,926
bookseller.

116
00:09:07,776 --> 00:09:12,891
He was in his sixties. He had come home
from the war, built an ordinary civilian

117
00:09:12,891 --> 00:09:18,061
life, and had almost certainly assumed
that the men he left on Lubang were dead.

118
00:09:18,061 --> 00:09:22,791
He was now being asked to fly to the
Philippines, put on his old uniform, and

119
00:09:22,791 --> 00:09:25,616
deliver an order thirty years late.

120
00:09:26,866 --> 00:09:28,506
He agreed.

121
00:09:29,556 --> 00:09:35,301
On the ninth of March 1974, in a clearing
on Lubang, Major Taniguchi read the formal

122
00:09:35,301 --> 00:09:41,109
order rescinding the 1944 one. The
Fourteenth Area Army was dissolved. All

123
00:09:41,109 --> 00:09:45,831
units were to cease operations. Onoda was
relieved.

124
00:09:46,881 --> 00:09:52,237
Onoda described what happened in him in
that moment as something coming apart. He

125
00:09:52,237 --> 00:09:56,819
had been carrying a mission for
twenty-nine years and it was taken off him

126
00:09:56,819 --> 00:09:58,485
in under a minute.

127
00:10:00,485 --> 00:10:05,201
He handed over his rifle, his sword,
around five hundred rounds of ammunition,

128
00:10:05,201 --> 00:10:09,901
and several hand grenades. The rifle was
still in working order.

129
00:10:09,951 --> 00:10:14,814
Two days later, in Manila, he presented
his sword to President Ferdinand Marcos,

130
00:10:14,814 --> 00:10:18,018
who pardoned him for the killings on
Lubang.

131
00:10:19,068 --> 00:10:25,028
He flew into Japan on the twelfth of March
1974, and the country lost its mind.

132
00:10:25,078 --> 00:10:30,217
Crowds met him at the airport. He was on
every front page. He was treated as a

133
00:10:30,217 --> 00:10:35,570
figure out of a vanished Japan. Proof, to
some people, of a loyalty the post-war

134
00:10:35,570 --> 00:10:38,490
country was thought to have abandoned.

135
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:40,937
He hated it almost immediately.

136
00:10:41,787 --> 00:10:46,826
The Japan he had been holding out for did
not exist. He had left a militarised

137
00:10:46,826 --> 00:10:51,691
empire in 1944 and come back to a
prosperous, democratic, American-allied

138
00:10:51,691 --> 00:10:56,962
consumer society in 1974. The country he
had killed for had become something that

139
00:10:56,962 --> 00:11:02,059
had no particular use for what he had
done. He was a celebrity, which is a

140
00:11:02,059 --> 00:11:05,189
different thing from being understood.

141
00:11:05,839 --> 00:11:10,557
He wrote a memoir. And within a year and a
half, he left.

142
00:11:11,107 --> 00:11:16,883
In 1975 he moved to Brazil, bought land,
and became a cattle rancher. He married.

143
00:11:16,883 --> 00:11:21,418
He stayed for most of a decade, in a
country with no opinion about the Imperial

144
00:11:21,418 --> 00:11:26,518
Japanese Army, doing physical work in a
place where nobody recognised him.

145
00:11:26,568 --> 00:11:30,668
He came back to Japan in the 1980s after
reading about a Japanese teenager who had

146
00:11:30,668 --> 00:11:34,769
murdered his parents, and he set up a
nature school for children. An

147
00:11:34,769 --> 00:11:39,569
organisation that taught camping,
survival, and self-reliance. He ran it for

148
00:11:39,569 --> 00:11:44,470
the rest of his life. Whatever he thought
he had learned on Lubang, that was what he

149
00:11:44,470 --> 00:11:45,920
did with it.

150
00:11:46,570 --> 00:11:52,009
In 1996 he went back to the island. He met
islanders whose families he had fought.

151
00:11:52,009 --> 00:11:56,633
His wife arranged a donation of ten
thousand dollars to a local school. The

152
00:11:56,633 --> 00:12:01,257
visit was not universally welcomed, and it
would be dishonest to present it as a

153
00:12:01,257 --> 00:12:06,479
reconciliation. For the families of around
thirty people, no visit and no donation

154
00:12:06,479 --> 00:12:08,406
closes the account.

155
00:12:09,856 --> 00:12:17,383
Hiroo Onoda died in Tokyo in January 2014,
of heart failure. He was ninety-one.

156
00:12:18,633 --> 00:12:23,607
Norio Suzuki, the young man who found him,
who went looking for a lieutenant, a

157
00:12:23,607 --> 00:12:28,636
panda, and a yeti, found the panda. He
died in 1986 in an avalanche in the

158
00:12:28,636 --> 00:12:31,533
Himalayas, still looking for the third.

159
00:12:33,933 --> 00:12:38,292
The thing that makes this story stick is
not the length of time. It is that Onoda

160
00:12:38,292 --> 00:12:40,919
was, in his own terms, right.

161
00:12:41,569 --> 00:12:46,286
He was told to hold out until he was
relieved. He held out until he was

162
00:12:46,286 --> 00:12:51,475
relieved. Every piece of evidence that the
war was over came from a source his

163
00:12:51,475 --> 00:12:56,723
training told him to distrust, and the one
source his training told him to trust was

164
00:12:56,723 --> 00:13:01,381
a man running a bookshop three thousand
kilometres away who had no idea he was

165
00:13:01,381 --> 00:13:03,028
still alive.

166
00:13:03,878 --> 00:13:09,411
The system that put him there had a way in
and no way out. It could issue him an

167
00:13:09,411 --> 00:13:14,447
order in 1944 and then simply stop
existing, and nothing in it accounted for

168
00:13:14,447 --> 00:13:18,154
the man still standing at the far end of
it.

169
00:13:19,204 --> 00:13:24,555
He was not mad. He was obedient, to an
institution that had dissolved, in a war

170
00:13:24,555 --> 00:13:29,754
that had ended, on an island where people
were still dying because of it.

171
00:13:31,204 --> 00:13:33,791
A bookseller had to get on a plane.

172
00:13:36,791 --> 00:13:41,867
The only thing that could end Hiroo
Onoda's war was the voice that started it.

173
00:13:41,867 --> 00:13:48,227
Not the truth, not the evidence, not his
own brother. An order. He kept faith with

174
00:13:48,227 --> 00:13:53,361
it for twenty-nine years, and the people
of Lubang paid for that faith with their

175
00:13:53,361 --> 00:13:59,313
lives. That is the whole story, and it is
a warning: loyalty is only ever as good as

176
00:13:59,313 --> 00:14:01,355
the thing it is loyal to.

